-// Chapter 33 checkpoint 2: the closest-hit shader reports the identity the traversal resolved.
+// Chapter 33 checkpoint 3: the closest-hit shader reports the geometry of the hit.
 //
-// A hit is not a boolean. The traversal that found it already knows which instance of the top-level structure it
-// belongs to, which identifier that instance carries, which triangle of the bottom-level geometry it is, and
-// which side of that triangle the ray arrived from, and DXR exposes each of those to the closest-hit shader as an
-// intrinsic rather than as something the shader searches for.
+// `RayTCurrent()` is the parametric distance of the committed hit along the ray. Every ray in this lab has a
+// unit-length direction, so it is a distance in metres, and every surface in the scene is a plane perpendicular
+// to the ray, so the values it is allowed to take are known before the frame runs.
 //
-//   * `InstanceIndex()` is the instance's position in the top-level structure and `InstanceID()` is the 24-bit
-//     value its instance description carries. This lab deliberately gives the two different values, so a record
-//     that reports both proves which of them a reader is looking at.
-//   * `PrimitiveIndex()` is the triangle's index inside its geometry, which is the only way to see that one
-//     bottom-level structure holds more than one triangle.
-//   * `HitKind()` is the object-space winding test D3D12 specifies: `HIT_KIND_TRIANGLE_FRONT_FACE` for a triangle
-//     whose vertices wind clockwise as seen from the ray, and `HIT_KIND_TRIANGLE_BACK_FACE` otherwise. It is
-//     decided from object-space vertices and the transformed ray; this scene's x mirror therefore preserves it.
+// The barycentric attributes are where inside the triangle the intersection landed. DXR hands a triangle
+// closest-hit shader a `BuiltInTriangleIntersectionAttributes` whose two floats are the pair (b1, b2) that
+// weights the vertices as v0 * (1 - b1 - b2) + v1 * b1 + v2 * b2. Only two of the three weights are stored
+// because the third is implied, which is why the attribute size the state object declares is eight bytes rather
+// than twelve.
 //
-// The distance, the barycentrics, the record the traversal selected and the colour it carries are still missing.
+// The record the traversal selected and the colour it carries are still missing, so every hit is still black.

 #include "../Common/DxrShared.hlsli"

